"Connects visible events with recurring patterns, underlying structures and mental models to deepen investigation of a persistent situation.\n\nUse when: Move a discussion beyond repeated reactions to isolated incidents. Generate questions about structural causes and governing assumptions.\n\nAvoid when: The team would present unsupported deeper explanations as proven causal facts.\n\nVisual grammar: Layered regions distinguish events, patterns, structures and mental models. A waterline metaphor separates easily observed features from less visible influences. Short evidence and question notes qualify each layer.\n\nBuild: Start with a concrete event and relevant repeated observations. Investigate structures that could generate the pattern. Surface plausible assumptions and beliefs, then identify evidence and learning actions.\n\nCheck: Pattern claims rely on more than one isolated occurrence. Structures refer to rules, incentives or relationships rather than vague blame. Mental models are explored respectfully as hypotheses.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming a deeper layer is automatically more truthful. Using the metaphor to bypass evidence about the actual situation.\n\nExample: A club investigates repeated volunteer shortages by reviewing scheduling patterns, recruitment rules and assumptions about who can participate.\n\nAlternatives: Causal-loop diagram, Rich picture."
